    <x:String x:Key="AnimationTitel1">Data type: Animation</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText1">GM1 animation files consist of a number of encoded images and 10 color tables. The color tables save space and provide the different player colors. The images shown use the currently selected color table:</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText2">Important: every pixel of an image stores a position in the color table, not a color. This position is the same for all color tables – only the table is exchanged, which produces the different player colors.</x:String>

    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText3">1 = gray/neutral??</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText4">2 = blue</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText5">3 = red</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText6">4 = orange</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText7">5 = yellow</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText8">6 = purple</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText9">7 = black</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText10">8 = turquoise</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText11">9 = green</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText12">10 = gray/neutral??</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="AnimationText13">Assignment of the color table numbers to the player colors / neutral colors:</x:String>

    <x:String x:Key="InterfaceTitel1">Data type: Interface</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="InterfaceText1">GM1 interface files consist of a number of encoded images. After the export these images can be edited completely, in size and in content. Unfortunately it is not yet known how Stronghold positions an image: the position stays fixed, so images can only be extended to the bottom and to the right.</x:String>

    <x:String x:Key="TiledObjectTitel1">Data type: TiledObject</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="TiledObjectText1">GM1 files of the type TiledObject (buildings) consist of a number of encoded images. In the GM1 file these images are made of many small diamonds of 30 × 16 pixels. The picture below shows such a diamond enlarged by a factor of 4.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="TiledObjectText2">The ground diamond of the picture is then built from these individual diamond tiles.</x:String>

    <x:String x:Key="TiledObjectText3">Finally, image parts of variable height are placed on the individual diamonds and the whole picture is put together.</x:String>

    <x:String x:Key="TiledObjectText4">This means that GM1 files of the type TiledObject can only be changed in height and in appearance. Everything below the ground diamond of the imported image is ignored, because this part has to be isometric.</x:String>

